WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:05.015
SugarPace. Your glucose and your fueling, on your Garmin Edge.

2
00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:13.555
Your latest reading from your own Nightscout site, color-coded by range, with the trend arrow and the age of the reading.

3
00:00:14.005 --> 00:00:18.629
A chart of the last four hours, and the foods you carry, one tap away.

4
00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:25.416
Tap a food tile to send a carb entry. It is secured by a one-time code.

5
00:00:25.866 --> 00:00:35.212
The tile shows the result: sending, sent, or failed with a short reason. Immediate repeat taps are ignored, to limit accidental duplicates.

6
00:00:36.712 --> 00:00:42.287
Behind the screen, your Edge uses your phone's connection to talk to your own Nightscout site.

7
00:00:42.737 --> 00:00:49.601
Glucose flows from your loop to Nightscout, then to your Edge. Carb entries flow back the other way, to your loop.

8
00:00:50.051 --> 00:00:54.427
SugarPace has no server of its own: nothing passes through the developer.

9
00:00:55.927 --> 00:01:00.928
The Treatments screen lists your temporary profiles. Tap one to activate it.

10
00:01:01.378 --> 00:01:07.894
If the data gets old, the number turns grey after fifteen minutes, so an old value never looks current.

11
00:01:09.394 --> 00:01:15.040
Optionally, you can allow sending the bolus that your loop recommends. It is off by default.

12
00:01:15.490 --> 00:01:26.105
It needs two taps and a recommendation less than ten minutes old, and an immediate repeat of the same one is blocked. If something goes wrong, the screen tells you to check your loop first.

13
00:01:26.555 --> 00:01:29.942
This is real insulin: always confirm on your loop.

14
00:01:31.442 --> 00:01:42.799
You need a Nightscout site, a loop that accepts remote entries, and a touchscreen Edge: 840, 850, 1040 or 1050.

15
00:01:44.199 --> 00:01:50.746
SugarPace is not a medical device. Always confirm values and decisions with your approved devices.

16
00:01:51.196 --> 00:01:56.470
Not affiliated with Garmin, Nightscout, or any loop app. Find it on GitHub.
